On Seaport, D's ideas add about the same saving at every level: -505 at `-O 1`, -629 at `-O s` (63,895 to 63,266), -469 at `-O 2` (63,751 to 63,282).

**Every level compiles.** Wall time of the whole `fe build` of the port: 418 s at `-O 1` and 617 s at `-O 2`, both measured with no other build of mine running, so `-O 2` costs about 1.5 times as long. That is well under the 3x threshold, so compile time does not obviously explain the choice of `-O 1`. Other runs overlapped with each other and with other agents' builds (`-O s` 850 s and 1,377 s, `-O 2` 1,446 s and 1,953 s), so those numbers are upper bounds, not comparisons. A second pinned `-O 2` build produced byte-identical output to the first.

**What differs between the levels.** Fe passes its level straight through (`to_sonatina_opt_level`, `crates/codegen/src/sonatina/mod.rs:223`). In Sonatina `3357f3a2`, `-O 1` and `-O 2` run the same IR optimization pipeline, `Pipeline::speed()`, which includes GVN, LICM, SCCP and CheckedArithElim (`crates/codegen/src/compile.rs:72,74`; the pass list is `PRIMARY_FUNC_PASSES`, `optim/pipeline.rs:277`). They differ only in the backend: `-O 1` uses a greedy stack-scheduling search and gas-oriented constants, `-O 2` an exact search and balanced constants (`compile.rs:266-275`). `-O s` runs `Pipeline::size()`, the same passes with a larger inlining budget (`size_inliner_config`, `optim/pipeline.rs:335`), plus the size-oriented late cleanup, the exact search, and size-oriented constants (`compile.rs:73,258,267,273`). So GVN does run at `-O 1`; #344 changed nothing because Seaport's code does not hit the phi pattern it fixes, not because `-O 1` skips GVN. (I measured B only at `-O 1`.)

## 2. Where Seaport's bytes go

Method: a measurement-only Fe patch of about 50 added lines (section 7) reads the per-instruction PC map that Sonatina already produces for its observability output (`PcMapEntry`, with `func_name`, `pc_start`, `pc_end`; `crates/codegen/src/object/artifact.rs:203` in Sonatina) and sums bytes per function. The per-function sums add up exactly to the section's code bytes (64,771 for A, plus 451 bytes of data = 65,222). Turning it on does not change the bytecode: the dumped Seaport runtime compares equal to the normal build for A, C and D `-O s`.

A function name with a `__g...` suffix is one generic instance; `__Seaport_recv_0_N` is one arm of the message dispatcher (the code that decodes one incoming call and runs its handler). `contract_runtime_root_Seaport` is the runtime entry point that reads the selector and jumps to the right arm.

