`apply_fulfillment` is not a separate function in A at all, and `match_advanced_orders` drops from 1,966 bytes in A to 1,003 here. My reading (not checked in the IR) is that at `-O 1` the inliner folded `apply_fulfillment` into `match_advanced_orders`, and at `-O s` the size-oriented inliner settings keep it out of line; the function count also goes from 210 to 213. Grouped: `decode_from_bounded` 4,979 (7.9%), dispatcher arms 3,570 (5.7%). Rollup: dispatch 9,531 (15.2%), ABI decoding 9,999 (15.9%), everything else 34,932 (55.6%).

What this says: there is no single bloated helper to fix. The largest wins would have to come from the shape of the code Fe emits (the dispatcher and ABI decoding together are about a third of Seaport), and the rest is spread over about 200 functions of order logic. The README's own note points the same way: seaport-core works on calldata pointers directly where the Fe port decodes into an in-memory model.

Raw per-function data: `/workspace/scratch/seaport/out/sizes-pinned-O1/`, `sizes-main/` (C, `-O 1`), `sizes-portopt-Os/`; each has `Seaport.runtime.tsv` and `Seaport.init.tsv`.

## 3. Gas

Seaport's Foundry suite runs without yarn: `scripts/upstream_suite.py` only needs `forge` and the reference artifacts, which `forge build` makes. I cloned the pinned Seaport reference (`7f966fe7`) with submodules and built its `reference` and `optimized` profiles with solc 0.8.24 (the Solidity `Consideration` runtime comes out at 23,969 bytes, as the README says). On this NixOS sandbox the solc 0.8.24 release binary needs a small loader wrapper (`/workspace/scratch/seaport/solc/solc-0.8.24`, sha256 of the binary matches the soliditylang release list).

Gas of the same calls in `test_fuzzCoverage_x` (from `forge test --mt test_fuzzCoverage_x -vvvv`, parsed by `/workspace/scratch/seaport/gas.py`). A's numbers reproduce the README gas table exactly.

